{
 "session": "58GEMSDOE session 6, 2026-10-11 (ribbon lane)",
 "hypothesis": "Matching the across-strike emission width to the MEASURED localisation error of the candidate ridge field pays more than any single fixed width: because the measured sigma is 3-9 px (i.e. of the same order as the 3-px scoring kernel radius), the true trace is usually NOT under the ridge centreline, so a tight line systematically misses it while a ribbon of parallel dotted lines spaced at the kernel radius spans the error; and because the Tversky penalty for a false positive is only alpha=0.2, spanning the error costs far less than the credit it recovers.",
 "mechanism": "19 organizer bands x 3 Hessian ridge scales (150/300/600 m) -> non-maximum-suppressed centrelines -> per-segment (block, family/sign, scale) stratum -> sigma from the holdout table -> K parallel dotted lines at +-3 and +-6 px across strike, one dot every 3 px along strike -> thin to strictly isolated pixels -> emit at exactly 1.0. Width choice evaluated with the shared exact metric, never a Normal approximation.",
 "non_fault_process_that_could_mimic_it": "Basin-and-Range alluvial-fan and terrace edges, and lithologic contacts in the magnetic/gravity fields (e.g. the edges of Tertiary volcanic packages against basin fill), produce continuous curvilinear ridges in every one of the families used; a Hessian ridge operator cannot distinguish a buried lithologic contact from a fault, so a 'ribbon' placed on one is a confident, well-localised line on a non-fault structure. The seismic and geodetic bands add a second mimic: strain-rate and earthquake-density gradients are smooth regional fields whose contours are also lineaments.",
